        Identifying a relaxing internal state in free-fall timing:
finite-frequency boundaries and an application to PSR J0337+1715

                                                 Juneyoung Kim

                                               28 September 2026

                                    Repository: lpaiu-cs/self-mass-unobservability

                                                                Abstract

             Proven. A body whose internal state relaxes on orbital timescales adds a pole to its re-
        sponse, but a timing measurement samples that response only at a few orbital carriers and
        only after nuisance fitting. We state what such a measurement can identify. A nonzero single
        pole can be matched at K positive carrier frequencies by a derivative comparator of degree N
        with unrestricted real coefficients if and only if N  2K - 1. A positive reciprocal relaxation
        spectrum whose rates exceed a gap obeys an inequality that a slow positive pole violates. For
        a positive reciprocal spectrum, two exact calibrated complex samples identify one observable
        relaxation time through a moment-variance equality, but finite precision cannot uniformly sepa-
        rate arbitrarily close poles, and a finite periodic response record does not determine the transient
        response. Counterexample candidate. A damped scalar-charge model supplies reciprocal
        forces and a controlled one-pole reduction. Imported from prior work. In a stored PSR
        J0337+1715 timing analysis, an archived drive dictionary violates the phase closure of the lead-
        ing physical drive, and the interval on the relaxation coefficient changes by factors of 2.1-17.4
        with the retained nuisance span. A calibrated six-coefficient confidence region gives 95.17-95.43
        percent inclusion on independent simulations in four specified covariance conditions. No relax-
        ation is detected, and timing-derivative and pulse-count checks prevent empirical promotion.
        Conjectural. The result is a set of identifiability boundaries and a conditional application, not
        an equation-of-state-matched or drive-independent SEP bound.

1 Introduction

Imported from prior work. Compact-body structure enters worldline effective field theory
(EFT) through body-dependent coefficients and, when needed, explicit internal degrees of freedom
[1, 2]. Internal modes appear as poles of response functions [3, 4], and nonadiabatic dynamical
scalarization supplies a monopolar example [5]. Tests of the strong equivalence principle (SEP) are
reviewed by Will [6]. The pulsar triple PSR J0337+1715 has provided sensitive tests of a static
SEP parameter [7, 8]; a later planet/noise analysis and public data release provide the setting for
the stored dynamic-template calculation used here [9, 10].

     Proven. A static description with finitely many operators at a fixed order does not make a
relaxing internal state unobservable, and a pole in a body's response does not by itself make that
state identifiable from timing. A timing measurement samples the response at a few orbital carriers,
after the nuisance directions of the timing model are projected out. Identifiability then depends on
the number of carriers, the comparator class admitted for the instantaneous response, the nuisance
span and the precision.

                                                                1
     Counterexample candidate. We use a one-state relaxation as the benchmark and a damped
scalar charge as its physical realization.

     Proven. For this benchmark we give an exact boundary for real derivative comparators that
interpolate a single pole at K carriers (Section 3.1), a comparator restriction from positivity and a
rate gap (Section 3.2), a two-sample identification of one observable relaxation time with its finite-
precision limit (Section 3.3), the insufficiency of a finite periodic record for transients (Section 3.4),
and a phase gate on the physical drive in J0337 (Section 4). The proofs use only elementary tools:
root counting for real polynomials, a variance inequality for a positive measure and an annihilating
differential operator. What is new is the set of identifiability boundaries they give for a relaxing
internal state in free-fall timing, not the tools. These results do not establish a new theory of
gravity or a general unobservability theorem.

     Imported from prior work. We apply these results to a stored J0337 analysis, exposing
the dependence of its interval on the nuisance span and calibrating its inference (Section 5). See
Supplemental Material at [URL will be inserted by publisher] for the complete technical account
(SM): a finite static operator catalog, the full audits and their provenance, and a worked calculation
for the inner white dwarf of the triple. The SM keeps its own section, equation, table and figure
numbering.

     Each scientific paragraph or result carries a status label. Proven denotes conditional math-
ematics, including numerical evaluation of explicit algebraic identities; Imported from prior
work denotes literature or numerical experiments recorded in the repository, including the audits
reported here; Counterexample candidate denotes a proposed physical/model realization; Con-
jectural denotes an interpretation requiring additional work. A theorem's premises are hypotheses
of its conditional statement, not independently established facts.

2 Benchmark and comparators

Counterexample candidate. Let a known dimensionless drive F excite a dimensionless state
and a scalar readout q:

 +  = F (t), q(t) = cY F (t) + c(t),  = c,  > 0,  (1)

with settled transfer function

                                                                         
                                                 G(i) = cY + 1 + i . (2)

    The readout may correct a specified observable coupling; assigning it to a mass or to a pairwise
gravitational parameter are different modeling choices. Relaxation presupposes an effective dissi-
pative setting. The general solution also contains he-(t-t0)/; the periodic analysis assumes that
this transient has decayed or is absent.

    Proven. A degree-N derivative comparator is a real polynomial PN (i), the transfer function of
a local readout nN anF (n) with real an. Expanding Equation (2) in i gives such comparators
with error at most ||N+1 on ||   < 1, so in the adiabatic regime a relaxation is absorbed
by a derivative comparator. The results below concern finitely many carriers at arbitrary .

                                2
